1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,425
A frog on the hostel steps.

2
00:00:02,425 --> 00:00:04,037
A lizard on the wall.

3
00:00:04,037 --> 00:00:05,548
A crow on the wire.

4
00:00:05,548 --> 00:00:07,674
A cat under the bicycle stand.

5
00:00:08,074 --> 00:00:09,854
All four have a backbone.

6
00:00:09,854 --> 00:00:11,574
All four are vertebrates.

7
00:00:11,574 --> 00:00:14,372
Yet each one belongs to a different class.

8
00:00:14,822 --> 00:00:19,273
Diya counted them on her way to class
and asked: what exactly makes a frog

9
00:00:19,273 --> 00:00:20,857
different from a lizard?

10
00:00:21,207 --> 00:00:24,886
The answer is in their skin, their
heart, and how they keep warm.

11
00:00:25,386 --> 00:00:30,129
By the end, you will know every vertebrate
class in NCERT, its key features,

12
00:00:30,129 --> 00:00:31,982
and the examples NEET loves.

13
00:00:32,632 --> 00:00:35,558
First, split the
vertebrates by one question.

14
00:00:35,558 --> 00:00:36,904
Does it have jaws?

15
00:00:37,254 --> 00:00:39,147
No jaws: the cyclostomes.

16
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:42,623
Lampreys and hagfishes,
with a round sucking mouth.

17
00:00:42,623 --> 00:00:45,380
They have no scales and no paired fins.

18
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:47,644
With jaws: everyone else.

19
00:00:47,644 --> 00:00:52,398
They split again into fishes with
fins, called Pisces, and the tetrapods.

20
00:00:52,398 --> 00:00:55,031
Tetra means four, and poda means feet.

21
00:00:55,481 --> 00:00:57,451
Fishes come in two classes.

22
00:00:57,451 --> 00:01:01,899
Tetrapods come in four: amphibians,
reptiles, birds and mammals.

23
00:01:02,549 --> 00:01:05,140
Let's meet each class, one body at a time.

24
00:01:05,490 --> 00:01:08,303
Chondrichthyes, the cartilaginous fishes.

25
00:01:08,303 --> 00:01:13,120
The skeleton is soft cartilage, the
mouth sits underneath, and the skin

26
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:14,921
has tiny placoid scales.

27
00:01:14,921 --> 00:01:17,479
Example: Scoliodon, the dog fish.

28
00:01:17,979 --> 00:01:20,052
Osteichthyes, the bony fishes.

29
00:01:20,052 --> 00:01:24,655
A bony skeleton, a flap called the
operculum over the gills, and an air

30
00:01:24,655 --> 00:01:25,555
bladder.

31
00:01:25,359 --> 00:01:27,284
Example: Labeo, the rohu.

32
00:01:27,784 --> 00:01:30,193
Amphibia live in water and on land.

33
00:01:30,193 --> 00:01:34,763
Moist skin with no scales, a tympanum
for an ear, and a common opening

34
00:01:34,763 --> 00:01:36,088
called the cloaca.

35
00:01:36,088 --> 00:01:37,969
Example: Rana, the frog.

36
00:01:38,469 --> 00:01:40,151
Reptilia creep or crawl.

37
00:01:40,151 --> 00:01:43,884
Dry, horny skin with scales,
and no outer ear opening.

38
00:01:43,884 --> 00:01:46,422
Example: Calotes, the garden lizard.

39
00:01:46,922 --> 00:01:48,215
Aves, the birds.

40
00:01:48,215 --> 00:01:52,656
Feathers, a beak, forelimbs turned
into wings, and long bones that are

41
00:01:52,656 --> 00:01:54,396
hollow, with air inside.

42
00:01:54,396 --> 00:01:56,330
Example: Corvus, the crow.

43
00:01:56,830 --> 00:01:57,730
Mammalia.

44
00:01:57,544 --> 00:02:01,895
Hair on the skin, outer ears called
pinnae, and mammary glands that make

45
00:02:01,895 --> 00:02:03,249
milk for the young.

46
00:02:03,249 --> 00:02:05,025
Example: Felis, the cat.

47
00:02:05,675 --> 00:02:06,919
Now, the heart.

48
00:02:06,919 --> 00:02:09,272
It changes as we climb the classes.

49
00:02:09,272 --> 00:02:10,468
Watch the blood.

50
00:02:10,818 --> 00:02:11,718
Step one.

51
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:16,163
A fish heart has two chambers: one
auricle that receives blood, and one

52
00:02:16,163 --> 00:02:18,093
ventricle that pumps it out.

53
00:02:18,843 --> 00:02:19,743
Step two.

54
00:02:19,714 --> 00:02:24,293
Amphibians and most reptiles have
three chambers: two auricles and one

55
00:02:24,293 --> 00:02:25,193
ventricle.

56
00:02:25,903 --> 00:02:26,848
Step three.

57
00:02:26,848 --> 00:02:31,376
Crocodiles, birds and mammals have
four chambers: two auricles and two

58
00:02:31,376 --> 00:02:32,276
ventricles.

59
00:02:33,009 --> 00:02:35,685
Birds and mammals are also warm blooded.

60
00:02:35,685 --> 00:02:38,412
Their body stays at the same temperature.

61
00:02:38,412 --> 00:02:41,640
Fishes, amphibians and
reptiles are cold blooded.

62
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:44,827
Their body warms and cools
with the surroundings.

63
00:02:45,477 --> 00:02:48,110
The two fish classes are easy to mix up.

64
00:02:48,110 --> 00:02:50,017
Put a shark next to a rohu.

65
00:02:50,367 --> 00:02:52,612
The shark's skeleton is cartilage.

66
00:02:52,612 --> 00:02:54,030
The rohu's is bone.

67
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,595
The shark's gill slits are
separate, with no cover.

68
00:02:57,595 --> 00:03:00,095
The rohu's gills hide under the operculum.

69
00:03:00,545 --> 00:03:02,701
And the shark has no air bladder.

70
00:03:02,701 --> 00:03:06,875
The rohu has one, and it controls
how high or low the fish floats.

71
00:03:07,525 --> 00:03:08,425
Your turn.

72
00:03:08,365 --> 00:03:11,996
Which one must keep swimming
all the time, or it sinks?

73
00:03:11,996 --> 00:03:13,735
The shark, or the rohu?

74
00:03:13,735 --> 00:03:14,971
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:18,171 --> 00:03:19,088
The shark!

76
00:03:19,088 --> 00:03:23,871
With no air bladder to keep it afloat,
a cartilaginous fish has to swim

77
00:03:23,871 --> 00:03:25,863
constantly to avoid sinking.

78
00:03:26,513 --> 00:03:27,456
Remember this.

79
00:03:27,456 --> 00:03:30,110
Two chambers, three
chambers, four chambers.

80
00:03:30,110 --> 00:03:32,879
Count the heart, and
you can place the class.

81
00:03:33,479 --> 00:03:35,223
Now, a worked example.

82
00:03:35,223 --> 00:03:37,763
Let's place Diya's four hostel animals.

83
00:03:38,113 --> 00:03:42,540
The frog has moist skin with no
scales, and it can even breathe through

84
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:43,440
that skin.

85
00:03:43,388 --> 00:03:44,288
Amphibia.

86
00:03:44,579 --> 00:03:47,671
The lizard has dry skin
covered with scales.

87
00:03:47,671 --> 00:03:48,571
Reptilia.

88
00:03:48,922 --> 00:03:52,359
The crow has feathers and
hollow, air filled bones.

89
00:03:52,359 --> 00:03:53,259
Aves.

90
00:03:53,369 --> 00:03:57,171
The cat has hair, outer ears,
and feeds its kittens milk.

91
00:03:57,171 --> 00:03:58,071
Mammalia.

92
00:03:58,550 --> 00:04:00,016
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:00,366 --> 00:04:03,568
Many students say the air
bladder helps a fish breathe.

94
00:04:03,968 --> 00:04:05,774
But fish breathe with gills.

95
00:04:05,774 --> 00:04:09,970
The air bladder regulates buoyancy:
it helps the fish float at the right

96
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:10,870
depth.

97
00:04:11,185 --> 00:04:12,524
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:12,524 --> 00:04:16,693
As match the following: a class with
its feature, or an animal with its

99
00:04:16,693 --> 00:04:17,593
class.

100
00:04:17,841 --> 00:04:19,040
Exam level now.

101
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:21,387
Match each class with its feature.

102
00:04:21,387 --> 00:04:22,637
Pause and solve.

103
00:04:28,037 --> 00:04:29,302
The answer is C.

104
00:04:29,802 --> 00:04:32,683
Claspers belong to cartilaginous fishes.

105
00:04:32,683 --> 00:04:35,461
The operculum belongs to bony fishes.

106
00:04:35,961 --> 00:04:39,446
Breathing through gills,
lungs and skin is amphibian.

107
00:04:39,446 --> 00:04:41,598
Pneumatic bones belong to birds.

108
00:04:42,198 --> 00:04:43,657
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:43,657 --> 00:04:45,156
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:48,356 --> 00:04:51,193
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:51,593 --> 00:04:52,493
One.

112
00:04:52,136 --> 00:04:56,002
What separates the cyclostomes
from all other vertebrates?

113
00:04:56,902 --> 00:04:57,802
Two.

114
00:04:57,392 --> 00:05:00,100
Which reptiles have a
four chambered heart?

115
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:01,900
Three.

116
00:05:01,601 --> 00:05:04,740
Name two differences
between a shark and a rohu.

117
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:10,540
Next time: a whale that is not a
fish, and a bat that is not a bird.

118
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:12,423
Who breaks the obvious rule?

119
00:05:12,423 --> 00:05:15,637
We find out in distinguishing
features and examples.
